  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1205 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nMonday and Tuesday will be seasonably colder with near to below normal\ntemperatures, dry conditions and light winds, as surface high pressure \nbuilds into the region. Highs look to be in the 30s/40s and lows in \nthe teens/20s. \n\nMid into late week the predictability of sensible weather is degraded, \nas deterministic models and ensembles diverge on the evolution of\nthe pattern. What seems fairly likely is that a northern stream \nshortwave will dive south from Canada into the central CONUS toward \nthe Thanksgiving holiday with some precipitation chances (mainly snow).\nNBM PoPs have trended higher over the past 24 hours for Wednesday night\nand Thanksgiving, and currently sit at 20-40% over much of the eastern\nhalf of the service area. Given the trends this is appearing to be the\nlower end scenario for us with a somewhat moisture starved digging\nshortwave bringing chances for showers (mainly snow), with confidence \non impacts being low given questions on the extent of moisture return\nand ground/pavement temperatures. In another scenario this aforementioned\nnorthern stream shortwave could interact/phase with a southern stream \nshortwave over the Lower Mississippi Valley. This in turn could lead \nto a stronger and wetter storm system. But, how much if any phasing\noccurs and where will be critical factors in determining the strength,\ntrack of any system over the eastern 1/3-1/2 of the CONUS. Bottom\nline, if you're planning any travel around Thanksgiving and just beyond,\nplease stay tuned to a trusted source for updates!",
